Both paragraphs contribute equally to the main scientific contribution of the parent segment, as they both describe different cases for the DenseSwap algorithm and its implications on ownership exchanges between agents A1 and A2. The first paragraph introduces the problem setup and outlines the three possible scenarios based on cut positions relative to the beads that need swapping. It sets up the foundation for understanding how the DenseSwap algorithm will function in different contexts, which is crucial for grasping the overall scientific contribution of this work.

The second paragraph delves into the details of each case introduced in the first paragraph. It explains the specific actions taken by agents A1 and A2 during ownership exchanges, including how they retain their original beads or intervals when no cuts need to be adjusted (first case), how extra unneeded beads are handled (second case), and how the fairness constraint is maintained throughout the process (third case). This paragraph also introduces Theorem 15, which confirms that DenseSwap produces a set of cuts with size exactly n(k−1) and has time complexity O(n).

Both paragraphs are essential for understanding the core scientific contribution of this work: the development and analysis of the DenseSwap algorithm. They provide complementary information, with the first paragraph setting up the problem context and the second paragraph detailing the specific actions taken by agents A1 and A2 in each case. Therefore, both paragraphs receive equal weightage in contributing to the parent segment's main scientific contribution.
